{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 25, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.3-70b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187 he was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. \n*Early Life* \nhe was born around 1140 and was the heir of his father Raymond II he became the Count of Tripoli after his father's death in 1152. \nRaymond III was a minor when he became the Count of Tripoli and the regency was held by King Baldwin III of Jerusalem. \n*Reign* \nRaymond III played an important role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem he was a strong supporter of King Amalric I of Jerusalem and participated in several military campaigns against the Fatimid Caliphate. \nHe also played a key role in the Battle of Inab in 1149 where the Crusader forces were defeated by the Zengid forces led by Nur ad-Din. \n*Capture and Later Life* \nRaymond III was captured by Nur ad-Din in 1164 and was held prisoner for several years he was released in 1173 after paying a large ransom. \nAfter his release Raymond III continued to play an important role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem he was a strong opponent of Guy of Lusignan and supported the claim of Raymond of Poitiers to the throne. \nRaymond III died in 1187 and was succeeded by his nephew Raymond IV Count of Tripoli. \n*Family and Legacy* \nRaymond III was married to Hodierna of Jerusalem but the marriage was childless he had several illegitimate children but none of them inherited his title. \nRaymond III is remembered as a strong and capable leader who played an important role in the history of the Crusader states. \nHe was a skilled military commander and a shrewd politician who was able to navigate the complex web of alliances and rivalries in the Middle East during the 12th century. \nRaymond III was also a patron of the arts and architecture and several buildings were constructed during his reign including the Church of Saint Ni